#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161735 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "42777bd2-44d0-5117-9e8b-ee722d8f3229" :question/math-academy-id "e-8276" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2 = 20.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=19x_1^2-6x_1x_2+11x_2^2.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/31f8c54fecdb8b2e8b3f3cad20a642a39eab9ae2987d49849a1c1dabc34e5368.png)\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 20\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I| = 0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=20 \\quad$ and $\\quad \\lambda_2=10.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/51897cac20d424f599eba1c8aada5259279ab9ad263e7557d6614ce79e68fa0f.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/f9913f9139af447be912bccc2350f086ce32bae89849495f8e82a3eebf8a7918.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}19 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 11\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/2cdd8527be173b17e254bd4ecda571d71b15938c3a1f6cb2609122db2b10c64d.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{10},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/f6764ef3200afce932740e8100915018b6a0c21e902d932b218e79bcd57e0b95.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/1549a73afb90f3fa0fb8218d9d63daa1a5534c3e546613cbf3f226371b8f1092.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/d4a6e3f0c138e6dd83252df8adc14359ba67ae77dec0270264ea6d00c7b08667.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161736 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "3564c452-1a51-5455-a6b1-223fda1611c0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8277" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2= 70?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=6x_1^2+8x_1x_2-9x_2^2.$ To  determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(\\mathbf{x})$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ which is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/33a30f2c07875359a60caed74f802a2de29ac9e44089800ed8f30a2015303bce.png)\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 4 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -70 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $7y_1^2 -10 y_2^2.$  \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}-10{y}_{2}^{2} & =70 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{10}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{7} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161750 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf2e573c-f351-5d5a-8e59-9c6532f1ab9d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Curve Using a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ec194ea7-df30-5370-b0c4-d27d4b34f8c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8473" :question/problem "Draw a diagram that depicts the lines representing the principal axes of the quadratic curve shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/5354b2fa6d2fb2c8b1b9ea3cb675225619e8debe64e1ec108b556248d6e672ec.png)" :question/worked-solution "The principal axes of a curve define a coordinate system in which the curve can be represented by its (simplest) canonical equation. \n\nOur curve is a hyperbola. This curve will have the canonical equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nif one axis passes through the vertices of the hyperbola and the other axis is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting the two vertices.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/bd0d117543012be1c4eab0acda878c9aea67518e46c93048e8c249de1a0c03e6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161773 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "ba46e82f-10b0-529c-94b0-3aebb7d1e1a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8678" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/4114e6536f57106949ed39a6ae4979646a0dd58c5690d7e7bcf9ffed15de8086.png) and the quadratic curve $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=1,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = 1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/771d020606de64641ab305039a0c9a9de926080e83707b270a333b5775a8e028.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/54a77383e863630dfd4e21a796801001aaed3084f1097bca62ef4a098e20b405.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/e0d71ad9216b9fa7c1831e6874b9a97bba9c1d9c5dbd6d9664635ed468d82a4c.png) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/83fd398b476c1c749346754a05c7f4071a98ab61453405a99ad8fcff65e38aaf.png)\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\qquad$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/8f0d84bb5c3ae37714c781b5dafbba1ae7de8561234ca558d3da2dcbfa9d75cf.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076805/assets/293ad98697d595e3e0049aae93e54209b6d17aa92eb131b57791f211167690fc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383530 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a727c0-0c69-5cde-bd49-a23ec0c6563f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383532} {:answer/id #uuid "6c994f35-b46d-5c1c-93e7-2ac8178cae09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383533} {:answer/id #uuid "aca36349-4f7f-536a-a6bd-a34442139674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383534} {:answer/id #uuid "757b6751-ea84-5cfc-8e9e-651cd3229119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383535} {:answer/id #uuid "499b271e-9581-5e77-9938-e2f3f220eb64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c994f35-b46d-5c1c-93e7-2ac8178cae09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383533} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df24f14-2ef2-533c-908d-480d4f782c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "042ead02-bb3a-5e4c-9374-7ae765cba5f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-140549" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}=24?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{6}$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82df2d8-739f-528c-9659-e7455314d874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161041} {:answer/id #uuid "dd714eb1-6ea2-5d75-a835-c733ffb76b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161042} {:answer/id #uuid "17a9b86b-069e-5b49-9c8c-32881eafb218" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $11$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161043} {:answer/id #uuid "35a98124-f296-5b5a-8584-d560183140f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161044} {:answer/id #uuid "6366b245-182f-5919-bd2d-fc4f9a968707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b82df2d8-739f-528c-9659-e7455314d874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161041} :answer-field/id #uuid "f74724ff-3a70-562a-91c3-99f5b0955233" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "151c3599-c63a-5e6a-8614-ca5bcf398293" :question/math-academy-id "q-94596" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $2{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+10{x}_{2}^{2}=44?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+10{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 10-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +11 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =11,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $11{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =44 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{44} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{11})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $2\\sqrt{11}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb78a5ca-9968-5401-bde1-d040679073ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383539} {:answer/id #uuid "8351a868-05fc-5924-82b5-3ba01988d0c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383540} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd6d3de-6aa3-59d5-b032-24a30d24dda9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383541} {:answer/id #uuid "1b925199-c569-5d41-ad1a-b87d9b7e6c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383542} {:answer/id #uuid "39da04af-7eca-5c70-b05c-63838b976a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb78a5ca-9968-5401-bde1-d040679073ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383539} :answer-field/id #uuid "51919194-e46e-5335-925b-d37134beb834" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383538}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "524143ac-0f98-5858-ae7f-d1be1f96a542" :question/math-academy-id "q-94997" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}=12?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e38b7ce-bd28-5611-984c-3d2523813823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $-1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383546} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c774e2-7fae-53ab-b632-2dbef3a59556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383547} {:answer/id #uuid "7375a74b-35c8-5078-82e7-cc4d5e1be5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle with radius $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383548} {:answer/id #uuid "68eac365-a32d-5074-bffc-d572275570ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383549} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff76bb0-e0a4-5f53-85a7-7bc7089dc663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ff76bb0-e0a4-5f53-85a7-7bc7089dc663" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383550} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ea237b0-048f-539e-b0c4-52571eb82fe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "749c0e99-8e51-5cee-8388-727f4d22f48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95000" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=8?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5d6ac06-40b4-5da0-bd26-860b35826292" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3ee7e10-e9c1-5269-b207-c0ec980965d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383553} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0cb43e-a7c0-5d13-b2f1-36f4a84cb03e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383554} {:answer/id #uuid "f7309976-06df-5972-9f81-e69407786d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle with radius $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383555} {:answer/id #uuid "8400b830-3a4b-5b22-8b86-72f5fc69dba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383556} {:answer/id #uuid "5676c7ce-c404-584d-914a-54f047309b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $3$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8400b830-3a4b-5b22-8b86-72f5fc69dba4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383556} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c34c9b-2fd8-5c84-b0b7-43c46106e09f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b7fa421-8274-5a15-90a7-f07c79709eb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-95015" :question/problem "Which curve is defined by the equation $-5{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}=12?$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=-5{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2}-6{y}_{2}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0a298d-5571-531f-9fe4-011fa09b2595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383560} {:answer/id #uuid "30d054d8-174c-5bf7-a614-fa857b7f80bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383561} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe70d8-f321-56af-b25c-a938a8c400e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383562} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f55f70-5733-5fe5-991f-0d992f212dbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383563} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7490ed-09c0-503c-b138-c76235d3fe39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1fe70d8-f321-56af-b25c-a938a8c400e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383562} :answer-field/id #uuid "e624583a-717d-5fd2-9584-d266520793cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d7d5dad8-80de-52d6-9f41-227da13cb052" :question/math-academy-id "q-95058" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}=100.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=100\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=25\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=20.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-25I & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{aligned}]-25[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-25I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=20,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-20I & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 21\\end{aligned}]-20[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-20I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ef7813-3304-557e-be6f-83b068a472f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383567} {:answer/id #uuid "07feeeca-fa62-5e55-bf81-939bd13666fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383568} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c47226-b411-5f15-a5b7-a7981300f27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383569} {:answer/id #uuid "89a50f3a-36c1-5bc5-a052-9a2f2d3fdf76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383570} {:answer/id #uuid "34e35e0b-21df-530b-9a3f-8844d4592dc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c47226-b411-5f15-a5b7-a7981300f27b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383569} :answer-field/id #uuid "3448c82c-50d9-5e2f-acfb-7cb4a8ea8c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fc392a3-f500-5b88-9a2e-df6cdd3a73e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-95805" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $9{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=5\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=10\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-7.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab47c137-7e18-5621-8368-5cd5c53f5dd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383574} {:answer/id #uuid "06dda8d5-74dc-56af-96a1-bece69eb2877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383575} {:answer/id #uuid "d09b551b-6f73-5a41-93da-519aab8797b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383576} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ddd895-787a-5cab-a4bf-9ef8e06933ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383577} {:answer/id #uuid "e72c7240-e7d8-5752-8639-1ac7d92ecee9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8ddd895-787a-5cab-a4bf-9ef8e06933ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383577} :answer-field/id #uuid "54127762-40ae-5140-bead-918a471f3ca3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "32b90dfa-ec90-5077-b365-15209caf0421" :question/math-academy-id "q-95806" :question/problem "Find the principal axes of the curve $2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}=9.$" :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6832be-62e1-506c-9b78-685f65362117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve"}] :question/answe