6a2508060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383624} {:answer/id #uuid "1f56209b-e044-532c-aeda-543574ca20de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383625} {:answer/id #uuid "83d3d754-4357-50e6-b7fc-6b21cebc59d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383626} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1e733a-2c19-5f3d-9098-9092978a7d91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f56209b-e044-532c-aeda-543574ca20de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383625} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc22569a-e1a1-5369-b389-2256bf925a0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "334c09b2-8068-5ab6-b14b-135919cc8d1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97988" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=2.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=2\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "400eb914-217f-5405-917b-8d6d946ec880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383630} {:answer/id #uuid "9b7ef421-b702-538d-9d34-6cd21eb503b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383631} {:answer/id #uuid "c89635a0-0f12-5653-a3f9-a4ccaca4b53b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383632} {:answer/id #uuid "1f3472a5-dea0-563e-a3ab-bb20e0af66a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383633} {:answer/id #uuid "77c371a6-5239-5a4b-a79c-ee4218709a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b7ef421-b702-538d-9d34-6cd21eb503b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383631} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce6ada2-20d0-5257-ae5c-8dd02ece0b85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fb95d9e-0ced-5515-baa2-9595c0717556" :question/math-academy-id "q-97990" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-3)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aa79f64-b1f9-5610-95b6-f8ea4874729b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383637} {:answer/id #uuid "716d146d-7c5a-5fdc-a578-baeffb2061fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383638} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb78adf-21cf-5c54-a56a-39998bdd78da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383639} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2d2304-9e8c-5dde-a2f8-e219e077476e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383640} {:answer/id #uuid "20791541-d42c-5701-8c8e-845582b4263f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8aa79f64-b1f9-5610-95b6-f8ea4874729b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383637} :answer-field/id #uuid "c93d706d-29b8-5548-9718-1e7d31668399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61a6fc08-cff1-54fa-821d-2b21fbaaba50" :question/math-academy-id "q-97992" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28e81aa0-9b57-5da1-a08d-47aa658d82bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383644} {:answer/id #uuid "53ed1257-38a4-5302-b106-f9e63e7370fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383645} {:answer/id #uuid "28c7b2c2-3c6c-5410-a33c-a17dced950e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383646} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cdf82f-3652-5af6-b2f6-cab5aabb90e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383647} {:answer/id #uuid "6e0abc6c-4803-5352-939f-8825d5da4cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e0abc6c-4803-5352-939f-8825d5da4cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383648} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dfa6d41-4cec-5f13-bf87-8b546dd46cd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a900debb-e366-52cb-8650-040664ad9bbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97993" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=3.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=3\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-7)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]+7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-7)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e97b8e36-3790-5cdc-bd77-417574af23f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Principal Axes of a Quadratic Plane Curve Given the Corresponding Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f0acab-0aed-5ed7-b13f-9a48fccfc50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383651} {:answer/id #uuid "450361ef-495d-5fa8-a436-c2b9522e261b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383652} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8f2b25-86ff-5300-a098-58fc7116357f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383653} {:answer/id #uuid "0043eda4-8d36-5567-86fe-0419ffc3d3db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383654} {:answer/id #uuid "a1be5595-2fbb-572c-9bc5-e3f089094222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "450361ef-495d-5fa8-a436-c2b9522e261b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383652} :answer-field/id #uuid "082c0e30-4b86-52a4-9f0f-9a514280ab40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "091c06c4-0cfd-5952-9df6-eba9b260f93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97995" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
